 "tastes": "<p>American IPA took the English idea of a strong, well hopped pale ale and rebuilt it around American hops: Cascade, Centennial, Simcoe, Citra and their relatives. These hops smell of grapefruit, orange peel, pine and resin, and the brewers used plenty of them in the kettle and again after fermentation.</p><p>The beer is gold to light amber and clear. Malt is a backdrop, a little bread and light caramel, just enough to carry the hops. Bitterness is firm and the finish is dry, which is what makes you want the next sip. A good one smells like you have just cut open a grapefruit in a pine forest. A tired one smells of cardboard and onion, because hop aroma fades fast.</p><p>The style spans a range. The West Coast version is the driest and most bitter. Others carry more malt. What they share is clarity and a bitter backbone, which is the clearest line between this and a hazy New England IPA. Drink it young and cold, and check the packaging date.</p>",
